<%# This screen drives htmx, so the layout loads it. Set here rather than in the routes
    because here is where the hx- attributes are: a partial that stops using htmx, or one
    that starts, changes its own answer in the same edit. A route flag would be a second
    place to remember, and the failure it produces is silent -- buttons that quietly stop
    swapping and fall back to full page posts. The same shape as @charts, which every view
    that plots something sets for the same reason. %>
<%- @htmx = true %>
<%# One table of workouts, rendered once for the sessions still ahead and once for
    training already done, so the two lists cannot drift apart in how a row reads. %>
<%# A card with three columns, since DESIGN.md: the date, which is the way into the session,
    the set count, and the two things you can do to a row. It was five columns -- Date, Sets,
    Show, Edit, Delete -- which made an admin table of a training log and spent a column on
    "Show" when the date was the obvious thing to tap. %>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto <%= card_style %>">
      <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200">
        <thead class="bg-gray-50">
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" class="py-3 pl-4 pr-3 text-left text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500 sm:pl-6">
              Session
            </th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-3 text-left text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">
              Sets
            </th>
            <th scope="col" class="py-3 pl-3 pr-4 sm:pr-6"><span class="sr-only">Edit or delete</span></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
          <%- workouts.each do |workout| %>
            <tr>
              <td class="whitespace-nowrap py-4 pl-4 pr-3 text-sm text-gray-500 sm:pl-6">
                <%# The day it was trained, falling back to the day it is written for. #572,
                    and Workout#performed_or_planned_on is the rule. Free of a query per row:
                    the route fetches this list `with_performed_on`, so the stamp is a column
                    on the row already and the fallback inside that method never fires here.
                    spec/query_count_spec.rb is what notices if that stops being true. %>
                <a href="/workouts/<%= workout[:id] %>/" class="font-medium text-lime-700 hover:text-lime-800">
                  <%= workout.performed_or_planned_on.strftime('%b %d, %Y') %>
                </a>
                <%# @today is the lifter's, not the server's (#349): on the server's clock a
                    Monday evening session in New York is already Tuesday and reads as missed
                    while it is being lifted. %>
                <%- if workout.status(@today) == :skipped %>
                  <span class="ml-1 rounded-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-gray-500">skipped</span>
                <%- end %>
                <%# The one thing this list could not say. Every session with a single set
                    ticked read the same as one taken to the last rep, because status is
                    derived and :performed means "something was lifted" -- so a day you
                    finished early and one you are still in the middle of were one row
                    apart and identical. #218.

                    Only on a session that says so, and quiet when it does not: an absent
                    badge means "not said", not "abandoned", and the app has no business
                    claiming the second. Grey rather than lime because lime already means a
                    completed set here and on the session screen, and this is a statement
                    about the session rather than a count of its rows. %>
                <%- if workout.finished? %>
                  <span class="ml-1 rounded-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-gray-500">finished</span>
                <%- end %>
                <%# Under the date rather than in a column of its own: most days have one
                    session and would leave that column empty, and the name is a caption on
                    the date rather than a fact standing beside it. Two sessions on one day
                    were the same date twice here, which is the other half of #143. %>
                <%- if workout.label %>
                  <div class="text-sm text-gray-900"><%= workout.label %></div>
                <%- end %>
                <%- if provenance(workout) %>
                  <div class="text-xs text-gray-500"><%= provenance(workout) %></div>
                <%- end %>
              </td>
              <%# set_count, not sets.count: the second walks the association and is a query per row,
                  which on this page is a query per session and grew with the history. It comes off
                  the row now, counted in the query that fetched the page (#234). %>
              <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-3 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500"><%= workout.set_count %></td>
              <%# Edit and Delete together at the end of the row, where the eye looks for what can
                  be done to a row rather than read about it. Show went: the date is the link now.
                  lime-700 is the link colour throughout the app -- 4.99:1 on white, the only
                  step in this scale that clears WCAG AA for body text. %>
              <td class="whitespace-nowrap py-4 pl-3 pr-4 text-right text-sm sm:pr-6">
                <div class="flex items-center justify-end gap-4">
                  <a href="/workouts/<%= workout[:id] %>/edit/" class="font-medium text-lime-700 hover:text-lime-800">Edit</a>
                  <form action="/workouts/<%= workout[:id] %>/delete" method="post"
                        hx-post="/workouts/<%= workout[:id] %>/delete"
                        hx-target="closest tr"
                        hx-swap="outerHTML">
                    <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{workout[:id]}/delete") %>
                    <button type="submit" class="font-medium text-red-700 hover:text-red-800">Delete</button>
                  </form>
                </div>
              </td>
            </tr>
          <%- end %>
        </tbody>
      </table>
</div>
